          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"verona-colei-che-non-si-deve-amare-fc3a71407483b37d\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.93,\"analysis\":\"The direct “ti amo” primarily avows the woman’s loving desire. Although embedded in an intensely sensual appeal for kisses and closeness, it does not itself undertake a commitment. The languorous, exhausted delivery supplies emotional intensity but no positive evidence that the words are a reflex-like discharge rather than an affectively charged avowal.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The flowing, semi-torpid delivery creates slight pressure toward an expressive reading, but the text does not depict involuntary blurting or loss of deliberative control.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"The woman directly tells Rigo “ti amo” during an intimate spoken appeal on the terrace.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The avowal forms part of a sustained erotic entreaty: she asks Rigo to kiss her, be gentle, and remain close, while the narration emphasizes languor, exhaustion, and mutual physical excitement. The later identification of Rigo as her brother makes the encounter transgressive and explains his conflicted withdrawal, but that narrative complication does not alter the utterance’s core force as an avowal of love and desire.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“ho tanto sonno... ti amo. Sii dolce con me...”\",\"supports\":\"The first-person present-tense declaration directly presents her love as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The declaration is surrounded by repeated requests including “báciami,” “Sii dolce con me,” and the observation that “nessuno ci guarda.”\",\"supports\":\"The utterance operates within an erotic appeal for immediate intimacy; these requests explain its persuasive context but do not turn “ti amo” itself into a relational undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narrator describes her as “snervata, spossata” and represents her speech with pauses and sleepy languor.\",\"supports\":\"The delivery is intensely affective, but there is no explicit indication that the words escape involuntarily or function independently as reflexive discharge; this supports E=0 under the calibrated criterion.\",\"confidence\":0.9},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately afterward Rigo kisses her frantically, then tears himself away; the wider passage later calls him “il fratello.”\",\"supports\":\"The avowal has powerful erotic and transgressive consequences, but those contextual features do not create an additional core-force category.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is marked as affirmative present Italian “ti amo” and reviewed as VALID_EXPLICIT_LOVE_I_YOU.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the highlighted occurrence is an explicit present-tense love declaration rather than a merely formal or accidental string match.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal. Its erotic purpose, heightened delivery, and transgressive relationship are adequately treated as context, without requiring P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
